  "full_response": "The **SOLID** principles are a set of five design principles in object-oriented software engineering that help create maintainable, scalable, and flexible code. They were introduced by **Robert C. Martin (Uncle Bob)** and are widely used in software development. Here’s a breakdown of each principle:\n\n---\n\n### **1. S – Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)**\n**Definition:**\n*A class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one job or responsibility.*\n\n**Key Idea:**\n- A class should focus on a single functionality.\n- If a class handles multiple responsibilities, changes to one may affect the others, leading to bugs.\n\n**Example:**\n❌ **Violation:**\n```java\nclass User {\n    void saveToDatabase(User user) { /* ... */ }\n    void sendEmail(User user) { /* ... */ }\n}\n```\n✅ **Solution (SRP-compliant):**\n```java\nclass UserRepository {\n    void saveToDatabase(User user) { /* ... */ }\n}\n\nclass EmailService {\n    void sendEmail(User user) { /* ... */ }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. O – Open/Closed Principle (OCP)**\n**Definition:**\n*Software entities (classes, modules, functions) should be **open for extension** but **closed for modification**.*\n\n**Key Idea:**\n- Extend behavior without modifying existing code (e.g., using interfaces or abstract classes).\n- Reduces the risk of introducing bugs in working code.\n\n**Example:**\n❌ **Violation:**\n```java\nclass Rectangle {\n    double width;\n    double height;\n}\n\nclass AreaCalculator {\n    double calculateArea(Object shape) {\n        if (shape instanceof Rectangle) {\n            return ((Rectangle) shape).width * ((Rectangle) shape).height;\n        }\n        // Adding a new shape (e.g., Circle) requires modifying this class.\n    }\n}\n```\n✅ **Solution (OCP-compliant):**\n```java\ninterface Shape {\n    double area();\n}\n\nclass Rectangle implements Shape {\n    double width, height;\n    public double area() { return width * height; }\n}\n\nclass Circle implements Shape {\n    double radius;\n    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }\n}\n\nclass AreaCalculator {\n    double calculateArea(Shape shape) {\n        return shape.area(); // No modification needed for new shapes.\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### **3. L – Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)**\n**Definition:**\n",
